r row id stays BLAKE3 and is refused explicitly otherwise. The install check keeps all three conjuncts in one `and`, so mixed-algorithm record and image both pass and no forged install passes on one conjunct alone.\n- The lint test file is untouched. The sites were fixed with the registry's total predicate, not by hiding the minting call behind an alias.\n- The required lane is green with the same counts codex reported. Kondo is clean on the four files. The slow goldens-match over every group passes.\n- The `[:c4 :py]` golden regenerates byte for byte through the documented method. The diff is fully explained by the two C4 prelude commits: `getattr` split into `lookup-attr` plus `getattr`, and twelve new definitions matching the report's list exactly. Only that group changed.\n- All nine mutants were killed and the source restored cleanly. Removing the row-algorithm refusal showed the pack is still refused later by canonical numbering, so that check names the defect early rather than closing a hole.\n\n**Findings**\n- F1 (medium): the non-BLAKE3 row id is refused as `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, but the UCF transport-tuples spec says `:yin.k/undecodable` in its row-id paragraph and `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` in step 4, which is what the code answered before. A third status was introduced without a spec amendment. Fix is one line either way: code follows the spec's dedicated paragraph, or the spec is amended in the same commit.\n- F2 (low): the new install test's negative case is caught by the record-address conjunct, not the image conjunct it targets, because the output was corrupted after the record address was minted. The existing custody test covers the image conjunct, which is what killed that mutant.\n- F3 (info): a comment note that numbering also refuses such packs.\n\nThe throwaway probe namespace is deleted; the worktree has no tracked changes. Nothing was committed.","ttft_ms":2211,"type":"result","duration_ms":579960,"uuid":"ea84bd0f-c04b-4d68-871c-d98e4691bf0f"